{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792923601,"nonce":"lt6atoP6woW1kbflWlgsqVX31xY=","title":"LSP: rayzor-lsp as a library with a module provider","message":"Part of 1626b90d930d209e848220743e00494408af449d2cdabdddea70f6c0a5337545 (caribou lsp).\n\nrayzor-lsp can only find a module that has a file under a class path. A\nmodule of another language, or a plugin's class, has no file. This is a\nchange in rayzor, so it is coordinated with the session that manages that\nrepository.\n\n- **A provider for missing modules.** The session asks a provider for any\n  dotted path its class paths do not have. The provider answers with Haxe\n  declarations as parser AST, or as source text with a path to report\n  against. The namespace resolver\n  (compiler/src/tast/namespace.rs, resolve_qualified_path_impl) and\n  read_source (compilation/sources.rs) consult it after the class paths.\n  The provider names its modules, so the session can re-check the documents\n  that import a provided module when it changes.\n- **Provided modules in the workspace index.** WorkspaceIndex lists provided\n  packages and types beside the files it walks, so completion offers them\n  in an import or a type position.\n- **An embedding API.** lsp/src/lib.rs exposes a session that a host builds\n  with its own provider and settings. It routes each document to the host\n  or to Haxe analysis by language, and lets the host publish diagnostics\n  for its own documents. Today only run_lsp, serve and spawn_session are\n  public.\n- **Skipping named initialization macros.** The host can name macros the\n  session must not run (caribou.Bridge.use()), since the provider supplies\n  what they would define.\n\nDone when a test session has a provider that declares `game.hud.Hud`,\nand a document that imports it type-checks with hover and completion on\nits members.","files":null}]}